## **9. Git Pull and Fetch Commands**

| Command                | Purpose                                 |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `git fetch`            | Download remote updates without merging |
| `git fetch origin`     | Fetch from origin                       |
| `git fetch --all`      | Fetch from all remotes                  |
| `git pull`             | Fetch and integrate remote changes      |
| `git pull origin main` | Pull main branch                        |
| `git pull --rebase`    | Pull using rebase instead of merge      |

## **10. Git Reset Commands**

| Command                     | Purpose                                   |
| --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| `git reset`                 | Unstage staged changes                    |
| `git reset --soft HEAD~1`   | Undo last commit, keep changes staged     |
| `git reset --mixed HEAD~1`  | Undo last commit, keep changes unstaged   |
| `git reset --hard HEAD~1`   | Undo last commit and discard changes      |
| `git reset commitID`        | Reset to a particular commit              |
| `git reset --hard commitID` | Reset working tree and branch to a commit |

**Remember:**

- `--soft` → Keeps changes staged.
- `--mixed` → Keeps changes unstaged.
- `--hard` → Discards changes.

Be careful with `--hard`.

## **11. Git Revert Commands**

| Command                     | Purpose                                    |
| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `git revert HEAD`           | Undo the latest commit safely              |
| `git revert commitID`       | Undo a particular commit                   |
| `git revert --no-edit HEAD` | Revert without editing the default message |
| `git revert --abort`        | Abort an ongoing revert                    |
| `git revert --continue`     | Continue after resolving conflicts         |

**Difference:**

- Reset changes commit history.
- Revert creates a new commit that reverses an earlier commit.

